  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nH5 ridging dominates the weather today, bringing much warmer\ntemperatures across western and central Wyoming. This will \nbring a beautiful autumn afternoon across Wyoming, with highs \nreaching the 60s for most areas. A weak trough sweeping by well \nto the north along the Canadian border will tighten the local \ngradient enough to ramp up winds this afternoon, generally from \nSouth Pass east across Natrona and Johnson Counties. Gusts in \nthese areas will reach 30 to 40 mph. This gusty wind will also \ntap some drier air across Johnson County, pushing temperatures \nthere even higher, reaching the mid to upper 70s.\n\nThe system to the north will bring a weak cold front down along\nthe I-25 corridor after sunset, bringing lighter north winds,\nand drawing temperatures back down closer to normal into the \nday Sunday. Highs will draw back down to the low 60s, similar to\nthe remainder of western and central Wyoming. \n\nModels are getting more consistent on the larger trough that\nwill be dropping southward along the West Coast this week. This\nwill put Wyoming in southwesterly flow between the trough and\nthe amplifying ridge across the Great Plains. A decaying cutoff\ntrough will wrap ahead of the diving coastal trough and zip\nnorthward Tuesday across western WY. Moisture with this\ndecreasing system is limited, so a few showers are possible\nTuesday afternoon, but any accumulations will be minimal at \nbest. The trough holds in place for a couple days across central\nCalifornia before ejecting to the northeast late in the week.\nWhere this trough moves northeast will have bearing on\nprecipitation chances and amounts when it moves through \nThursday and Friday.",
